# Single source of truth for every pinned version this dev container builds
# from — base image digest, apt packages, the mise installer, every mise-managed
# tool, and the npm-global CLIs. See AGENTS.md, "Toolchain (mise)".
#
# NOT A SECRET. This file is deliberately committed (.gitignore negates the
# bare `.env` rule for it). It is only named `.env` because Docker Compose
# auto-loads a file with exactly that name from beside docker-compose.yml —
# which is what lets docker-compose.yml's `build.args: { NAME: ${NAME} }` reach
# the Dockerfile's `ARG NAME` with no --env-file flag and no custom tooling.
#
# TWO consumers read this one file:
#   1. Docker Compose  → Dockerfile build ARGs (base image, apt, npm globals)
#   2. mise            → tool versions, via `[env] _.file` + {{ env.X }}
#                        templating in mise.toml at the repo root.
#
# Format is dotenv: KEY=VALUE, no `export`, no quotes needed. Every key here is
# exported into the shell environment of the container by mise's `_.file`, so
# treat these names as occupying the global env namespace.
#
# TO BUMP ANYTHING: edit the value here, then run
#   mise lock -p linux-x64,linux-arm64,macos-arm64,macos-x64
# and commit .devcontainer/.env together with mise.lock. CI fails the PR if the
# lockfile is not in sync (a bumped version with a stale lock has no reviewed
# checksum to verify against; `pnpm verify:mise` is the gate).
#
# Most keys below no longer need that by hand: Renovate opens the PR and runs
# that exact `mise lock` command inside it, so .devcontainer/.env and mise.lock
# arrive in one commit. Which keys are covered is visible here — a `# renovate:`
# annotation directly above a key means it is; no annotation means it is not,
# and the comment there says why. See ADR-0019 and docs/repository-setup.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base image
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Digest-pinned: tags are mutable, digests are not. Re-resolve when bumping:
#   curl -sI -H "Accept: application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json" \
#     https://mcr.microsoft.com/v2/devcontainers/base/manifests/ubuntu24.04 \
#     | grep -i docker-content-digest
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ANNOTATED. Renovate could bump this digest — it has a docker
# datasource — but the apt pins below are read out of THIS image and must move
# with it, and Renovate has no apt datasource to move them. Every monthly digest
# bump would open a PR that fails the build on a stale apt pin. Bumping both by
# hand, prompted by that same build failure, is the smaller job. See ADR-0019.
BASE_IMAGE_TAG=ubuntu24.04
BASE_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:cfd5dd36c0d0d88de015aad5aa4f4b94e76d9f658a1e3055b27fd7d0fd1d84a6

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apt packages
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned exact against the archive for the digest above (read from `apt-cache
# policy` in that exact image, not copied guesses). Bump together with
# BASE_IMAGE_DIGEST: when a superseded security patch drops out of the archive
# the pinned install fails, and that failure IS the signal to refresh both.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ANNOTATED: Renovate has no Ubuntu apt datasource, so these
# are unreachable by design rather than by choice. The build failure above is
# the mechanism.
#
# Deliberately short. devcontainers/base:ubuntu24.04 already ships git, zsh,
# gnupg2, openssh-client, ca-certificates, curl, wget, net-tools, iproute2,
# lsof and jq. Re-pinning what is already present would only add stale-pin
# build failures. These are what neither the base image nor mise provides:
#   build-essential  C toolchain for node-gyp native deps
#   zlib1g-dev       GraalVM `native-image` links against libz at build time
#   unzip / zip      archive handling for tooling and CI scripts
#   iputils-ping     ping (absent from the base image)
#   dnsutils         dig / nslookup, for debugging service resolution
#   netcat-openbsd   nc, for "is that port actually open" checks
#   traceroute       path/MTU debugging
APT_BUILD_ESSENTIAL_VERSION=12.10ubuntu1
APT_ZLIB1G_DEV_VERSION=1:1.3.dfsg-3.1ubuntu2.1
APT_UNZIP_VERSION=6.0-28ubuntu4.1
APT_ZIP_VERSION=3.0-13ubuntu0.2
APT_IPUTILS_PING_VERSION=3:20240117-1ubuntu0.1
APT_DNSUTILS_VERSION=1:9.18.39-0ubuntu0.24.04.5
APT_NETCAT_OPENBSD_VERSION=1.226-1ubuntu2
APT_TRACEROUTE_VERSION=1:2.1.5-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mise itself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The installer is pinned too — an unpinned `curl https://mise.run | sh` is the
# one unverified binary in an otherwise fully-pinned image, and it is the tool
# that installs everything else.
#
# The `# renovate:` line below (and above every pin in the next section) is what
# makes this file machine-updatable: a custom manager in .github/renovate.json
# matches exactly that comment plus the KEY=VALUE under it. A pin with no
# annotation is invisible to Renovate and is bumped by hand — see ADR-0019.
# Note the value keeps its `v` prefix here, so this one uses github-tags with no
# extractVersion; the toolchain pins below are bare and strip it.
# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jdx/mise
MISE_VERSION=v2026.8.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Toolchain — consumed by mise.toml via {{ env.X }}
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exact patches, never fuzzy majors: `mise lock --bump` once resolved a fuzzy
# "24" to 24.16.0 while 24.18.1 was current, silently leaving the container two
# patches behind.
# Kept in lockstep with .nvmrc — Renovate groups the two into one PR (`node`).
# renovate: datasource=node-version depName=node
NODE_VERSION=24.18.1
# Kept in lockstep with scripts/.python-version — grouped the same way.
# renovate: datasource=python-version depName=python
PYTHON_VERSION=3.14.6
# GraalVM CE (not Oracle): GPLv2+CPE is unambiguous for a template others fork.
# native-image ships inside the distribution as of 21 — no `gu install` step —
# and links against libz, hence APT_ZLIB1G_DEV_VERSION above. Also serves as
# SonarLint's language-server JRE, which requires a real JRE 21+ path.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ANNOTATED. The value is a mise/asdf java plugin identifier,
# not a version string: upstream tags this release `jdk-21.0.2`, and no Renovate
# datasource emits `graalvm-community-21.0.2`. An annotation here would resolve
# against the wrong shape and propose an unbuildable value. Bump it by hand.
JAVA_VERSION=graalvm-community-21.0.2
# Kotlin compiler and Gradle. Until these landed the JVM side of the toolchain
# was a runtime only — GraalVM was installed to give SonarLint's language server
# a JRE, and nothing could actually compile. Both run ON the GraalVM above
# rather than fetching a JDK of their own, which is what JAVA_HOME in the
# Dockerfile pins down.
#
# Kotlin 2.x targets JVM 21 fine, so this pair does not force the LTS above to
# move. Check that again before bumping to a Kotlin major that raises its
# minimum target.
#
# GRADLE_VERSION is a fallback, not an override. The Java extension's
# `java.import.gradle.wrapper.enabled` defaults true, so any project that
# commits a gradlew still builds with the wrapper's own version. This is what a
# wrapper-less project — or the `gradle init` that CREATES the wrapper — gets.
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=JetBrains/kotlin extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
KOTLIN_VERSION=2.4.10
# renovate: datasource=gradle-version depName=gradle
GRADLE_VERSION=9.6.1
# Rust toolchain (rustc + cargo), for building Rust apps in this repo.
#
# THE ONE TOOL mise.lock CANNOT CHECKSUM. mise's `core:rust` backend delegates
# to rustup, which resolves and downloads toolchains itself, so mise never sees
# an asset URL: `mise lock` reports "0 platform entries (4 skipped)" and writes a
# version-only entry. The asdf:code-lever/asdf-rust backend does exactly the
# same — both were tested before settling for it. Integrity is rustup's job
# here (signed channel manifests over TLS), not mise.lock's.
#
# That exception is NAMED in scripts/verify-mise-lock.mjs (NO_PLATFORM_CHECKSUMS)
# rather than being a silent pass, and `pnpm verify:mise` prints it on every run.
# The version half of the gate still applies: bumping this line without
# re-running `mise lock` still fails CI.
# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=rust-lang/rust
RUST_VERSION=1.97.1
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=astral-sh/uv
UV_VERSION=0.12.0
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=cli/cli extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
GH_VERSION=2.96.0
# MUST match the image tag in .github/workflows/security.yml — `pnpm verify:mise`
# fails if it does not, because a drifted pair breaks nothing loudly. The pre-commit
# hook and the CI job are the same scanner on the same default ruleset (there is
# no .gitleaks.toml — neither side overrides it), and they only stay the same
# scanner if this number and that tag move together. Renovate reaches both — this
# key and that image tag+digest — and .github/renovate.json groups them into one
# PR, because two PRs that each pass alone would fail the moment either merged.
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=gitleaks/gitleaks extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
GITLEAKS_VERSION=8.30.1
# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=aws/aws-cli
AWSCLI_VERSION=2.36.14
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=zzet/gortex extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
GORTEX_VERSION=0.63.0
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=anomalyco/opencode extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
OPENCODE_VERSION=1.18.10
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=rtk-ai/rtk extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
RTK_VERSION=0.44.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sidecar images — consumed by docker-compose.yml
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Digest-pinned, not tag-pinned, and `pnpm verify:deps` enforces that: a tag is
# mutable, so `postgres:17` is a different filesystem next month and "it broke
# and nothing changed" becomes unanswerable for your data layer too. The tag is
# kept as a trailing comment because that is what Renovate reads to bump it —
# same convention as a workflow `uses:` ref.
#
# Re-resolve a digest after changing a tag:
#   docker buildx imagetools inspect <image>:<tag>
#
# POSTGRES: major 17 because that is what Supabase runs (their self-hosted
# default moved 15 -> 17 in June 2026), and this template's recommended hosted
# path is Supabase. Matching majors locally means the same SQL, the same
# extensions and the same dump/restore behaviour in both places. The database
# INSIDE it is named `postgres` for the same reason — see docker-compose.yml.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ANNOTATED for a version bump: Renovate's docker datasource
# would happily move this to 18 on its own, and a Postgres major upgrade is a
# data migration, not a version bump. Move it by hand, together with whatever
# Supabase is running.
POSTGRES_IMAGE_TAG=17
POSTGRES_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:7958605b474b3d264a969cb3a123d6aa00ad1e1fe9da8a69984dabb704d93317

# ADMINER: the web database UI, reachable at http://localhost:8080 once the
# container is up. Chosen over a VS Code extension because it is a ~160 MB
# Docker Official Image with no licence tier and no per-editor setup.
#
# VERIFIED driver list in this image — enumerated from its own login page, not
# from the marketing copy: MySQL/MariaDB, PostgreSQL, SQLite, MS SQL,
# Oracle (beta). That is the SQL family only.
#
# Elasticsearch, MongoDB and Redis are NOT built in. They exist as Adminer
# plugins and need enabling by mounting them into /var/www/html/plugins-enabled,
# which is a real step, not a checkbox. Budget for it when one of those actually
# lands here — and re-evaluate then, since dbgate/dbgate (GPL-3) covers Redis
# and MongoDB natively but has no Elasticsearch either. No single free tool
# currently covers Postgres + Elasticsearch + Redis in one UI.
ADMINER_IMAGE_TAG=latest
ADMINER_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:890cffec7caa20159fb6f68c1a521b2e5879f7314f4845d4ebca7cc1cf145971

# Local-only Postgres password. NOT A SECRET and deliberately trivial: this
# database is bound to the dev container, holds nothing real, and is recreated
# from migrations. A realistic-looking password here would be worse — it would
# train people to expect credentials in this file and give gitleaks a reason to
# start alerting on it. Real environments get theirs from the platform.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# npm-global CLIs — installed by the Dockerfile, NOT by mise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mise's npm backend installs with --ignore-scripts / --omit=optional, so this
# package's postinstall never runs and its platform-native binary is never
# fetched; `claude` then fails at runtime with "native binary not installed".
# `npm install -g` runs the postinstall properly. Verified the hard way.
# renovate: datasource=npm depName=@anthropic-ai/claude-code
CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION=2.1.220

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# devcontainer Features
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authoritative pin lives in .devcontainer/devcontainer-lock.json (version +
# integrity digest); this copy exists so a human scanning "what is pinned
# where" sees it in one place. Keep the two in sync when bumping.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ANNOTATED, and Renovate's devcontainer manager is disabled in
# .github/renovate.json for the same reason: the authoritative pin is a lock
# entry Renovate cannot regenerate. It would bump the `:4` selector in
# devcontainer.json and leave devcontainer-lock.json resolving the old digest —
# a half-applied bump that still builds, from a lock nobody reviewed. Bump both
# by hand with `devcontainer upgrade`, or from the values that command prints.
DOCKER_IN_DOCKER_FEATURE_VERSION=4.0.0
